lemma_76.statement:
(The exact obstruction to (GC): no Frobenius-stable Jordan block of size two, and the multiplicity count.) Let \ell be a prime, put R := \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t^{\pm 1}], let V be a nonzero finite-dimensional \mathbb{Q}_\ell-vector space, let T \in \mathrm{GL}(V), and regard V as an R-module by t\cdot v := T(v). For a monic irreducible f \in \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t] dividing \chi_T, let e_f := v_f(\chi_T) be the exponent of f in \chi_T and define the TOTAL multiplicity m_f := e_f \cdot \deg f = \sum_{\alpha:\,f(\alpha)=0} \mathrm{mult}_\alpha(\chi_{T_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_\ell}}) \;(= \dim_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V[f^{\infty}]); write V[f] := \ker f(T), V[f^2] := \ker f(T)^2 and V[f^{\infty}] := \bigcup_{N\ge1}\ker f(T)^N. Prove, from linear algebra over \mathbb{Q}_\ell only and without reproving lemma_60, lemma_68 or lemma_74:
(i) one always has \dim_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V[f] \le m_f, and T is semisimple on V if and only if equality holds for every f \mid \chi_T; more precisely \dim_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V[f] = m_f if and only if T is semisimple on the f-primary piece V[f^{\infty}]. (Note: with m_f read as the exponent e_f the inequality is FALSE for deg f > 1 — e.g. V = \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t]/(f) with f irreducible quadratic and T = multiplication by t gives \dim V[f] = 2 > 1 — so the total-multiplicity normalisation above is essential; for deg f = 1, and hence over \overline{\mathbb{Q}}_\ell, the two readings agree and (i) says that the number of Jordan blocks at a root is at most its multiplicity in \chi_T, with equality iff T is semisimple.)
(ii) T is semisimple if and only if V contains NO R-submodule isomorphic to R/(f^2) for any monic irreducible f \in \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t];
(iii) equivalently, T is NOT semisimple if and only if there exist a monic irreducible f \mid \chi_T and vectors x, y \in V with y \neq 0, f(T)y = 0 and f(T)x = y, i.e. a T-stable two-step chain inside one f-primary piece;
(iv) BLOCK COUNT: after extending scalars to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}_\ell, f splits into distinct roots (characteristic zero), and \dim V[f^2] - \dim V[f] equals the total number of Jordan blocks of T of size \ge 2 belonging to the roots of f; in particular T is semisimple on the f-primary piece V[f^{\infty}] if and only if V[f^2] = V[f];
(v) GEOMETRIC PACKAGING: let (GC_2) be the statement that for every m \ge 1, every smooth projective geometrically connected Y over \mathbb{F}_{q^m}, and every n \ge 0, writing V := H^n_{\mathrm{et}}(Y_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_q},\mathbb{Q}_\ell) and T := arithmetic Frobenius over \mathbb{F}_{q^m}, the equivalent conditions (i)-(iv) hold (with m_f the total multiplicity defined above). Prove that (GC_2) is equivalent to (GC), the hypothesis of lemma_59, and hence that (GC_2) implies the conjecture. State the geometric target explicitly as: there is no Frobenius-stable Jordan block of size >1 in any primary piece of any H^n.

lemma_247.proof:
{
  "lemma_id": "lemma_247",
  "cited_lemmas": [
    {"user_id": "eoinm", "lemma_id": "lemma_77"}
  ],
  "cited_references": [],
  "proof": "Let k = algebraic closure of F_q. Let c_L in CH^1(X) be the algebraic first Chern class of L; its etale cycle class is c_1(L). For Z = X,Y write cl_Z for cycle classes. For a 0-cycle delta on a scheme over a field F, write deg_F(delta) for its Chow degree. 1. Reduction to a scalar. By lemma_77(a), for Z=X,Y the trace isomorphism H^{2d}_{et}(Z_k,Q_ell) -> Q_ell(-d), d=dim Z, after tensoring with Q_ell(d), gives a Q_ell-linear isomorphism deg_Z: H^{2d}_{et}(Z_k,Q_ell(d)) -> Q_ell such that deg_Z(cl_Z(z)) = [kappa(z):F_q] for every closed point z. Hence V_Y := H^{2E}_{et}(Y_k,Q_ell(E)) is one-dimensional. The functional Phi(u)=deg_X(c_1(L)^r cup f^*u) is Q_ell-linear, so Phi = lambda deg_Y for a unique lambda in Q_ell. It remains to show lambda is a positive integer. 2. Generic fiber and restriction map. Since X and Y are smooth and connected over a field, they are integral; f is dominant. Put K=kappa(Y), eta=Spec K, X_eta=X times_Y eta, L_eta=L|_{X_eta}. The generic fiber X_eta is integral: locally it is the localization of a domain at the nonzero elements of the coordinate ring of an affine open of Y. Its function field is kappa(X), so dim_K X_eta = trdeg_K kappa(X)=D-E=r. It is projective over K: choose m>>0 such that L^m is very ample, giving a closed immersion i:X -> P^N_{F_q}; since Y is separated, (i,f):X -> P^N_{F_q} times Y is a closed immersion, and base change to K gives a closed immersion X_eta -> P^N_K with (L^m)_eta=O(1)|_{X_eta}. Thus L_eta is ample. For every i>=E define rho_i:CH_i(X)->CH_{i-E}(X_eta). On a prime cycle [W], with W subset X integral of dimension i, set rho_i([W])=[W_eta] if f(W)=Y, and rho_i([W])=0 otherwise, where W_eta=W times_Y eta. If f(W)=Y, then W_eta is integral of dimension i-E; when i=E it is Spec kappa(W). Well-definedness. Let W' be integral of dimension i+1 and g in kappa(W')^*. If W' is not dominant, no point of W' maps to eta, so rho_i(div(g))=0. If W' is dominant, W'_eta is integral with function field kappa(W'), and its points are exactly the points q in W' mapping to eta. For such q the local ring of W'_eta at the corresponding point is canonically isomorphic to O_{W',q}; hence the coefficient of q in div(g) equals the coefficient of the corresponding point in div_{W'_eta}(g). Therefore rho_i(div(g))=div_{W'_eta}(g)=0 in CH_{i-E}(X_eta). Thus rho_i descends to rational equivalence. Compatibility with Chern classes. For i>=E+1 and an integral W of dimension i, choose a nonzero rational section s of L|_W. Then c_L cap [W]=[div(s)]. If W is not dominant, both rho_i(c_L cap [W]) and c_{L_eta} cap [W_eta] are zero. If W is dominant, s_eta is a rational section of L_eta|_{W_eta}, and div(s_eta) is the restriction of div(s) to the points over eta; hence rho_i(c_L cap [W])=c_{L_eta} cap [W_eta]. By linearity, rho_i(c_L cap alpha)=c_{L_eta} cap rho_i(alpha) for all alpha in CH_i(X). Applying this repeatedly to [X] gives rho_E(c_L^r cap [X])=c_{L_eta}^r cap [X_eta]. Relation with pushforward. For an integral E-dimensional W subset X, f_*[W]=[kappa(W):K][Y] if f(W)=Y, and f_*[W]=0 otherwise. Also deg_K rho_E([W])=[kappa(W):K] in the dominant case and 0 otherwise. Therefore, if alpha in CH_E(X) and f_*alpha=n_alpha[Y], then n_alpha=deg_K rho_E(alpha). Put beta=c_L^r cap [X] in CH_E(X), and let n in Z be defined by f_*beta=n[Y]. Then n=deg_K(c_{L_eta}^r cap [X_eta]). 3. Positivity of n. We prove a general fact: if Z is an integral projective scheme of dimension s over a field F and A is ample on Z, then I(Z,A):=deg_F(c_1(A)^s cap [Z]) is positive. If s=0, Z=Spec L for a finite extension L/F and I=[L:F]>0. Assume s>0. Replace F by the infinite purely transcendental extension F'=F(t). Put Z'=Z times_F F' and A'=A_{F'}. The scheme Z' is integral: on an affine open Spec B with B a domain, B tensor_F F(t)=S^{-1}B[t] is a domain. Chern classes and degrees of 0-cycles are compatible with field extension: for a prime 0-cycle [p] on Z, deg_{F'}([p]_{F'})=dim_{F'}(kappa(p) tensor_F F')=dim_F kappa(p)=[kappa(p):F], so I(Z',A')=I(Z,A). Choose m>0 such that A'^m is very ample, and embed Z' into P^M_{F'}. Set Z_0=Z'. Inductively choose F'-rational hyperplanes H_1,...,H_s such that H_j does not contain any associated point of Z_{j-1}; this is possible because F' is infinite and the set of associated points is finite. Let Z_j=Z_{j-1} cap H_j. Since H_j avoids associated points, H_j|_{Z_{j-1}} is an effective Cartier divisor and c_1(O(H_j)) cap [Z_{j-1}]=[Z_j], where [Z_j] is the divisor cycle. As O(H_j)|_{Z'}=A'^m, induction gives c_1(A'^m)^s cap [Z']=[Z_s]. Each Z_j is nonempty of dimension s-j: if Z_j were empty, then Z_{j-1} would be contained in the affine complement P^M minus H_j, which is isomorphic to A^M_{F'}; a proper affine F'-

scheme is finite, contradicting dim Z_{j-1}=s-j+1>0. Hence Z_j is nonempty and has dimension s-j. Since H_j avoids the associated points of Z_{j-1}, the linear equation of H_j is a nonzerodivisor on Z_{j-1}; by the principal ideal theorem the effective Cartier divisor cycle [Z_j] is nonzero and has dimension s-j. Induction gives that [Z_s] is a nonzero effective 0-cycle on Z'. Its degree over F' is positive because it is a sum of prime 0-cycles with positive integer coefficients, each of which has positive degree. Since O(H_j)|_{Z_{j-1}} is the restriction of A'^m, the cap-product identity gives c_1(A'^m)^s cap [Z']=[Z_s]. Also c_1(A'^m)=m c_1(A'), so m^s I(Z',A')=deg_{F'}[Z_s]>0. Thus I(Z',A')>0, and by compatibility of Chern classes and 0-cycle degrees with field extension, I(Z,A)>0. Applying this to Z=X_eta, F=K, A=L_eta, s=r gives n>0. For r=0, the same positivity fact gives n=deg_K[X_eta]=[kappa(X):K]>0. 4. Identification of lambda. Choose an ample line bundle M on Y defined over F_q, and let c_M be its algebraic first Chern class. Set u_0=c_1(M)^E in H^{2E}_{et}(Y_k,Q_ell(E)). By the positivity fact, deg_Y(u_0)=deg_{F_q}(c_M^E cap [Y])>0. The class u_0 is the cycle class of c_M^E cap [Y]. By functoriality of cycle classes with pullback and Chern classes, f^*u_0=cl_X(c_{f^*M}^E cap [X]). Hence c_1(L)^r cup f^*u_0=cl_X(c_{f^*M}^E cap beta), where beta=c_L^r cap [X]. Let gamma=c_{f^*M}^E cap beta in CH_0(X). By the normalization of deg_X on closed-point classes, Phi(u_0)=deg_X(cl_X(gamma))=deg_{F_q}(gamma). For any 0-cycle delta on X, deg_{F_q}(f_*delta)=deg_{F_q}(delta), because for a closed point w mapping to y, f_*[w]=[kappa(w):kappa(y)][y] and degrees of residue fields multiply. Therefore deg_{F_q}(gamma)=deg_{F_q}(f_*gamma). By the projection formula in Chow groups, f_*gamma=f_*(c_{f^*M}^E cap beta)=c_M^E cap f_*beta=n(c_M^E cap [Y]). Thus Phi(u_0)=n deg_{F_q}(c_M^E cap [Y])=n deg_Y(u_0). But Phi=lambda deg_Y, and deg_Y(u_0) is nonzero, so lambda=n. Set d=n. Then d is a positive integer and Phi(u)=d deg_Y(u) for every u in H^{2E}_{et}(Y_k,Q_ell(E)), which is the desired identity."
}
